window.docContents[30].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Pure-tensors-and-tensor-products-of-multiple-spaces/#Chapter2___Discussion_pure_tensors":{"contents":"The elements v \\otimes w \\in V \\otimes W, for v \\in V, w \\in W are called pure tensors. Note that in general, there are elements of V \\otimes W which are not pure tensors.\nThis allows one to define the tensor product of any number of vector spaces, V_1 \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes V_n. Note that this tensor product is associative, in the sense that (V_1 \\otimes V_2) \\otimes V_3 can be naturally identified with V_1 \\otimes (V_2 \\otimes V_3).\n\nIn particular, people often consider tensor products of the form V^{\\otimes n} = V \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes V (n times) for a given vector space V, and, more generally, E := V^{\\otimes n} \\otimes (V^*)^{\\otimes m}. This space is called the space of tensors of type (m, n) on V. For instance, tensors of type (0, 1) are vectors, tensors of type (1, 0) — linear functionals (covectors), tensors of type (1, 1) — linear operators, of type (2, 0) — bilinear forms, tensors of type (2, 1) — algebra structures, etc.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products\u0009Pure tensors and tensor products of multiple spaces","header":"Pure tensors and tensor products of multiple spaces","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Pure-tensors-and-tensor-products-of-multiple-spaces/#Chapter2___Discussion_pure_tensors"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/Ext___1-for-polynomial-algebras-and-algebras-with-zero-multiplication/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___9___-Problems--Ext___1-for-polynomial-algebras-and-algebras-with-zero-multiplication--Ext___1-for-polynomial-algebras-and-algebras-with-zero-multiplication--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary algebra type indexed by a natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe module associated with evaluation at a tuple of complex scalars.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe complex polynomial algebra with a finite family of variables.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nA two-dimensional complex module over the polynomial algebra has a submodule equivalent to one evaluation module whose quotient is equivalent to another.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nWhen there is more than one generator, there is a natural-number-indexed family of auxiliary modules satisfying the displayed predicate and pairwise nonisomorphic over the auxiliary algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nPolynomial scalar multiplication on an evaluation module agrees with multiplication by the evaluated scalar.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.9. Problems\u0009Ext^1 for polynomial algebras and algebras with zero multiplication\u0009Ext^1 for polynomial algebras and algebras with zero multiplication\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/Ext___1-for-polynomial-algebras-and-algebras-with-zero-multiplication/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___9___-Problems--Ext___1-for-polynomial-algebras-and-algebras-with-zero-multiplication--Ext___1-for-polynomial-algebras-and-algebras-with-zero-multiplication--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/#chapter-04___section-4-9":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results","header":"4.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/#chapter-04___section-4-9"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Algebraic-representations-and-highest-weight/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Definition5___23___1":{"contents":"Note that subrepresentations, quotients, direct sums, tensor products and duals of algebraic representations are algebraic. For example, V^{\\otimes n} and hence all L_\\lambda are algebraic. Also define L_{\\lambda - r \\cdot 1^N} := L_\\lambda \\otimes (\\wedge^N V^*)^{\\otimes r} (this definition makes sense by Proposition 5.22.2). This is also an algebraic representation. Thus we have attached a unique irreducible algebraic representation L_\\lambda of GL(V) = GL_N to any sequence (\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_N) of integers (not necessarily positive) such that \\lambda_1 \\geq \\cdots \\geq \\lambda_N. This sequence is called the highest weight of L_\\lambda.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$\u0009Algebraic representations and highest weight","header":"Algebraic representations and highest weight","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Algebraic-representations-and-highest-weight/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Definition5___23___1"}});